{"body":"## Background\n\nThe current iroh configuration in `crates/radicle-artifact-node/src/iroh.rs` stacks two address lookup services. `presets::Minimal` only installs the rustls crypto provider — it does *not* set discovery, a relay mode, or a `DnsResolver`. Everything else is added explicitly, and since we never call `.dns_resolver(...)`, the resolver falls back to the system default (`DnsResolver::new()` → system `/etc/resolv.conf`, else Google `8.8.8.8`, over plain UDP).\n\n## How the current config behaves\n\n`address_lookup()` does `Vec::push`, so the services coexist, each playing a distinct role (each implements only one half of the trait). We currently configure:\n\n| Service | Role | Transport |\n|---|---|---|\n| `PkarrPublisher` | publishes this node's info | HTTPS PUT to `pkarr_url` |\n| `DnsAddressLookup` | resolves peers | DNS TXT at `dns_endpoint_origin` |\n\nNote we do **not** currently configure a `PkarrResolver`, so the only thing on the encrypted (pkarr/HTTPS) path today is publishing — *peer resolution is entirely plain DNS*.\n\n- `PkarrPublisher` announces our endpoint to the pkarr relay. By default its `AddrFilter` publishes only the `RelayUrl`, not direct IPs (to avoid leaking addresses to a public relay).\n- `DnsAddressLookup` is the read side for discovering other endpoints.\n\n### Resolution at runtime\n\nWhen more than one resolver is configured, `AddressLookupServices::resolve` queries them concurrently and merges outputs into one stream (`MergeBounded`):\n\n- The first usable address is yielded as soon as it arrives — we act on it while a slower resolver is still in flight.\n- A failure from one resolver is yielded inline and does not abort the others; `NoResults` only occurs if all services fail to produce anything.\n- This matters if we add a `PkarrResolver`: it (direct to origin, freshest, HTTPS) and `DnsAddressLookup` (through the DNS hierarchy, cacheable, plain DNS) would race — we'd get whichever lands first, with the other as redundancy.\n\n## What's encrypted vs. not (today)\n\n- ✅ Pkarr publish → HTTPS to `pkarr_url`\n- ❌ Peer resolution → plain system DNS (UDP), via `DnsAddressLookup` TXT queries\n- ❌ Resolving the `pkarr_url` hostname itself → plain system DNS (the `PkarrPublisher` gets the endpoint's default `DnsResolver` injected at build time)\n- ❌ Resolving custom relay URL hostnames → plain system DNS (the relay client uses the same default `DnsResolver`)\n\nSo discovery has no encrypted resolution path today — only publishing rides over HTTPS.\n\n## Options\n\nThere are a couple of ways to encrypt endpoint discovery:\n\n- Add a `PkarrResolver` (and drop `DnsAddressLookup`). Endpoint resolution then goes over HTTPS to the pkarr server.\n- Configure a `DnsResolver` with only HTTPS nameservers. See `Endpoint::builder().dns_resolver()` and `DnsResolver::builder().with_nameservers`.\n\n## Maintainer insight\n\nThe iroh maintainer shared the following:\n\n- A DoH-only `DnsResolver` applies to *all* DNS resolution in iroh. Apart from endpoint address lookup, this includes looking up relay URL domains and pkarr server addresses (for publishing).\n- DNS lookup with a DoH resolver goes to the configured DoH server, which then forwards the query to the upstream DNS server. It can be faster if the server is close to you and has records cached. That caching can also be unwanted if you are dealing with frequently changing information. The `PkarrResolver` goes directly to the origin server, so it always gets the latest info, at potentially higher latency.\n\n## Proposed direction\n\nLean towards adding a `PkarrResolver` (replacing `DnsAddressLookup`) so that endpoints are resolved in an encrypted fashion, while the relay and pkarr endpoints are still resolved using system DNS. This gives us the freshest, lowest-trust path for the data that actually matters, without forcing all DNS in iroh through a single DoH server.","embeds":[],"id":"2c4c1a753fd531cfd3dd0e6f1f6500d9c41fc26a","type":"comment.edit"}